---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import CodeTabs from "../components/CodeTabs.astro";

// The surface guide: the third of the four ways. It is deliberately not in the
// same shape as Parley or Morphorm, because a surface is not a seam — there is
// nothing to implement and nothing to install. The two doors (`surface` and
// `gpu_canvas`) and the two transports (same-device texture, exported handle)
// are the whole of the API, and the producers out of tree are what make it
// useful.
//
// Facts and figures below are from the 1.25 line: `surface()` and `gpu_canvas()`
// in `gpui_authoring`, `SurfaceSource`/`PaintSurface` in `gpui_engine`, and the
// export-cost band from `gpui_interop`'s `interop_bevy` example.

const stats = [
  {
    value: "2",
    label: "Doors",
    note: "surface() and gpu_canvas()",
    win: true,
  },
  {
    value: "3",
    label: "Transports",
    note: "CoreVideo, Direct3D, dma-buf",
  },
  {
    value: "0",
    label: "Seams replaced",
    note: "It sits above the facade",
  },
  {
    value: "0.15–0.8 ms",
    label: "Export cost",
    note: "Measured by interop_bevy",
  },
];

const transports = [
  {
    platform: "macOS · iOS",
    source: "CoreVideo",
    payload: "CFRetained<CVPixelBuffer>",
    note: "an IOSurface-backed image buffer",
  },
  {
    platform: "Windows",
    source: "DirectX",
    payload: "DirectXSource",
    note: "a Direct3D 11 texture, or a view a producer already made",
  },
  {
    platform: "Linux",
    source: "DmaBuf",
    payload: "DmaBufHandle",
    note: "an fd plus a DRM fourcc, modifier, stride and offset",
  },
];

const consumers = [
  {
    name: "gpui_interop",
    badge: "public · 0.1",
    does: "Bridges a producer's own device: dma-buf on Linux, a shared texture and fence on Windows, IOSurface planned on macOS. It is the crate that makes an external renderer or decoder a surface source.",
    facts: [
      "Vulkan, wgpu, Direct3D 12 producers",
      "the same-device fast path as well as the export",
      "input and guest-mode follow-ons live here",
    ],
    href: "https://github.com/bite-gpui/gpui_interop",
    linkLabel: "bite-gpui/gpui_interop",
  },
  {
    name: "gpui_video",
    badge: "public · pre-release",
    does: "A hardware-decoded video player on the same path: a VA-API decoder renders into driver-tiled NV12 surfaces, exports them as dma-bufs, and the element paces playback with HTML5-shaped controls.",
    facts: [
      "Linux / VA-API is the implemented backend",
      "macOS and Windows designed, not built",
      "not published under these names",
    ],
    href: "https://github.com/bite-gpui/gpui_video",
    linkLabel: "bite-gpui/gpui_video",
  },
  {
    name: "interop_bevy",
    badge: "example",
    does: "A Bevy scene inside a GPUI window, with Bevy built headless: no winit, no window, no event loop of its own. GPUI owns the cadence and Bevy renders into the window renderer's own device.",
    facts: [
      "one Bevy tick per GPUI paint",
      "paint_texture by default; --transfer for the dma-buf shape",
      "~4–6 ms/frame on the test laptop",
    ],
    href: "https://github.com/bite-gpui/gpui_interop/tree/main/examples/interop_bevy",
    linkLabel: "examples/interop_bevy",
  },
];

const surfaceCode = `<span class="k">use</span> gpui::{<span class="t">ObjectFit</span>, surface};

<span class="c">// a decoded frame, a shared texture, or a dma-buf you already hold</span>
<span class="k">let</span> picture = <span class="f">surface</span>(source)
    .<span class="f">object_fit</span>(<span class="t">ObjectFit</span>::<span class="t">Contain</span>);`;

const canvasCode = `<span class="k">use</span> gpui::{<span class="t">GpuCanvasContext</span>, gpu_canvas};

<span class="k">let</span> viewport = <span class="f">gpu_canvas</span>(|gpu: &amp;<span class="k">mut</span> <span class="t">GpuCanvasContext</span>| {
    <span class="c">// \`frame\` is a SurfaceSource from your producer</span>
    gpu.<span class="f">paint_surface</span>(frame);
});`;

const textureCode = `<span class="k">use</span> gpui::{<span class="t">Corners</span>, gpu_canvas};
<span class="k">use</span> gpui_wgpu::<span class="t">WgpuRenderer</span>;

<span class="k">let</span> viewport = <span class="f">gpu_canvas</span>(|gpu| {
    <span class="c">// the device and queue this window already draws with</span>
    <span class="k">let</span> (device, queue) = gpu.<span class="f">device</span>::&lt;<span class="t">WgpuRenderer</span>&gt;();

    <span class="c">// draw into a texture on \`device\`, then composite it</span>
    gpu.<span class="f">paint_texture</span>(handle, <span class="t">Corners</span>::<span class="f">default</span>(), <span class="n">1.0</span>, <span class="k">false</span>);
});`;
---

<Page
  title="Bring your own surface — bite-gpui"
  description="Composite a 3D viewport, a decoded video frame, or a texture you computed into a GPUI window through surface() and gpu_canvas(), with no CPU copy and no seam to implement."
  kicker="Ways to extend · Shove"
  heading="Bring your own"
  accent="pixels."
  lede="A surface is content GPUI did not draw: a video frame from a hardware decoder, a 3D viewport on its own device, or a texture computed off-thread. surface() and gpu_canvas() composite it as an ordinary element — no seam to implement, no install hook, no CPU round-trip."
  current="shove"
  stats={stats}
>
  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="kicker">Two doors</span>
      <h2>What you already hold decides the door.</h2>
      <p>
        If your producer already has a handle GPUI can import, hand it to
        <span class="mono">surface()</span>. If it needs the window's own device
        to make one, open a <span class="mono">gpu_canvas()</span> and paint
        through the context it gives you.
      </p>
    </div>

    <CodeTabs
      panes={[
        { label: "surface(source)", html: surfaceCode },
        { label: "gpu_canvas(|gpu| …)", html: canvasCode },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Both return ordinary elements. <span class="mono">surface()</span> takes
        a <span class="mono">SurfaceSource</span> and fits it into the element's
        bounds with the same <span class="mono">ObjectFit</span> modes an image
        uses. <span class="mono">gpu_canvas()</span> is a
        <span class="mono">Div</span> in every respect — styling, layout and
        interactivity are forwarded to the box — whose paint phase adds one
        primitive after the box. There is no
        <span class="mono">with_surface</span> and nothing to install.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="kicker">What travels</span>
      <h2>One enum, cfg-gated to the platform.</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="mono">SurfaceSource</span> names only the transport this
        build is compiled against, so the engine stays free of every graphics
        API but one. Each variant reaches the renderer as a view it samples; the
        renderer cannot tell how the view was made.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-card">
      <div class="table-head">
        <span class="table-head-title">The transports</span>
        <span class="table-head-note">one variant per platform</span>
      </div>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>platform</th>
              <th>source</th>
              <th>payload</th>
              <th>what it is</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {transports.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td><span class="mono">{row.platform}</span></td>
                <td><span class="mono">{row.source}</span></td>
                <td><span class="mono">{row.payload}</span></td>
                <td>{row.note}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="table-note">
        A <span class="mono">PaintSurface</span> carries the source with its
        bounds, content mask and draw order. Its
        <span class="mono">to_quad_record</span> is the same quad record a
        styled element builds, which is why a surface clips and stacks like any
        other layer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="kicker">Same device or exported handle</span>
      <h2>The short path, and the portable one.</h2>
      <p>
        A producer that renders on the window renderer's own device never leaves
        it: <span class="mono">paint_texture</span> samples the texture view and
        queue ordering is the only synchronization. A producer with its own
        device exports an OS handle and a fence, and
        <span class="mono">paint_surface</span> imports it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <CodeTabs panes={[{ label: "Same device", html: textureCode }]} />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        <span class="mono">gpui_wgpu</span> is the published
        <span class="mono">bite-gp-wgpu</span>. On Apple the renderer type is
        <span class="mono">MetalRenderer</span> and on Windows
        <span class="mono">DirectXRenderer</span>; the device each lends is that
        backend's own handle.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          <strong>The same-device path is the default for a reason.</strong>
          There is no export, no fence and no hand-off. It is what
          <span class="mono">interop_bevy</span> uses: Bevy renders into an
          image on the window renderer's wgpu device and GPUI samples its view.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The exported path costs a hand-off per frame.</strong> In the
          Bevy example, exporting the dma-buf and importing it on the consumer
          side adds <strong>~0.15–0.8 ms/frame</strong>; the producer's own
          timings barely move, which is why the cost shows up on a tight frame
          budget rather than in a throughput number.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The device is the renderer's, not a new one.</strong>
          <span class="mono">device::&lt;R&gt;()</span> returns the handle the
          window already draws with — a <span class="mono">metal::Device</span>,
          an <span class="mono">ID3D11Device</span>, or a wgpu device and queue
          — and <span class="mono">try_device</span> is the fallible form for a
          producer that can degrade instead of asserting the backend.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="kicker">Who does it</span>
      <h2>The producers are crates, not features.</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing about a surface names a video decoder or a 3D engine. The crates
        that do are public and sit above the facade, exactly where the
        architecture puts them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card-grid three">
      {consumers.map((consumer) => (
        <article class="card">
          <div class="card-head">
            <h3 class="card-title mono">{consumer.name}</h3>
            <span class="card-badge">{consumer.badge}</span>
          </div>
          <p>{consumer.does}</p>
          <ul class="card-facts">
            {consumer.facts.map((fact) => (
              <li>{fact}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <a
            class="card-link"
            href={consumer.href}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            {consumer.linkLabel} →
          </a>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The same primitive carries four kinds of work: hardware video (a decoder
        exports NV12 buffers), embedded 3D viewports (a renderer on its own
        device), emulators and capture pipelines (frames leave another process),
        and computed textures (a simulation or a chart rendered off-thread).
        None of them is a seam in this engine; all of them are a source the
        renderer samples.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="kicker">What it is not</span>
      <h2>Not a swap, and not a wrap.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          <strong>No trait is implemented.</strong> A swap implements a seam and
          is installed at bootstrap; a surface does not replace a shaper, a
          solver or a pipeline. It adds a primitive to a frame.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No frame is decorated.</strong> A wrap forwards passes; a
          surface is painted by the element that owns it and is done before the
          frame's own bookkeeping.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It sits above the facade.</strong> The commitment is to the
          published payload types a source is built from — the token and the
          handle — not to a trait that would freeze a backend.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not copy through the CPU.</strong> That is the whole
          point. A producer that can only hand over bytes should upload them
          itself; a surface exists for the cases where the pixels never need to
          leave the GPU.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="kicker">Boundaries</span>
      <h2>What a producer has to get right.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          <strong>The decoder must share the renderer's GPU.</strong> A dma-buf
          is importable only on the GPU that exported it, so a VA-API device and
          the wgpu renderer must be the same physical card.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The handle has to match what the renderer imports.</strong>
          A dma-buf declares its DRM format and modifier; a Direct3D texture
          must be shareable; an IOSurface is the Apple equivalent. The importer
          refuses what it cannot sample rather than guessing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cross-API means a fence.</strong> Two devices, two queues: the
          producer exports a fence with the frame and the consumer waits on it.
          On the same device, queue ordering is the fence.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The platform matrix is not symmetric yet.</strong> Linux and
          Windows have the full imported path; macOS has the CoreVideo variant
          and <span class="mono">gpui_interop</span>'s IOSurface support is
          planned.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-title">The rest of the ways</span>
      <p>
        <a href="/swaps">Ways to extend</a> has all four, and
        <a href="/serve">Bring your own platform</a> is the host side. If what
        you want to change is the algorithm a seam runs, that is a
        <a href="/swaps#swap">swap</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
